# Copy to .env, fill in, then `docker compose up -d`.
#
# Nothing here is required to bring the stack up. Everything about the app —
# the TMDb token, the library folders — is configured from the Settings page
# and stored in the database, because the one setting between a fresh install
# and a working library should not need a restart. What is here is the part
# that spans two containers, and so has to be agreed in advance.

# --- Jackett ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where Jackett keeps its config. Point this at an install you already have and
# its indexers and settings carry straight over; leave it unset and the stack
# uses docker/jackett-config/ and starts fresh.
JACKETT_CONFIG=/Users/mohanraj/.config/Jackett

# Normally Jackett invents this on first start and you go and fetch it. Set it
# here instead and both sides are told: the key is written into Jackett's config
# before it boots, and handed to the app at the same time. Nothing to paste.
#
# Generate your own — this one is an example, and an example is a published key:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
JACKETT_API_KEY=5efa8f4481c26fd4e8e2091692c3ebf1

# The service name, on the network the two of them share.
JACKETT_URL=http://jackett:9117

# Leave both blank to configure Jackett from Settings by hand instead. Blank is
# read as "not set". Either way Settings has the last word: what you save there
# is used in place of these, and disconnecting there comes back to them.

# A fresh Jackett also gets three public indexers to search — The Pirate Bay,
# TorrentDownload and LimeTorrents — so the Queue works on the first page load.
# Only ever on a fresh one: once Jackett has indexers of its own, the set is
# yours. Set to 0 to start empty and choose your own.
JACKETT_SEED_INDEXERS=1

# --- Local ------------------------------------------------------------------
TZ=Europe/London

# Where the app answers on this machine. Not 3000: that is `next dev`, and the
# container and the dev server should be able to run at the same time.
RIPGRADE_PORT=6969

# Who owns Jackett's config files. `id -u` and `id -g` — on macOS, 501 and 20.
# Ignored on a bind mount, which keeps the ownership it has on the host.
PUID=501
PGID=20

# Where the folder picker opens. Only change this if you bound the drive
# somewhere other than /Volumes — the paths in an existing database are
# absolute, and they were written on the host.
RIPGRADE_BROWSE_ROOT=/Volumes

# --- Not here -----------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no VPN section, and that is deliberate. The trackers are blocked by
# name, not by address, so the stack runs CoreDNS forwarding over TLS instead —
# see docker/Corefile and docs/Indexer_Connectivity_Fix.md. A VPN would also
# have worked, at the cost of a subscription and signing the machine out of
# everything else.
